[Music]  this morning amanda gorman will become  the youngest known  inaugural poet when she performs at the  59th  presidential inauguration as the  nation's first youth poet laureate  you may recognize gorman she's performed  five commissioned poems  for cbs this morning over the past few  years today  she'll deliver her original composition  the hill we climb  at the capitol in front of joe biden  kamala harris  and the entire nation we spoke to her  ahead of her big moment  in the declaration's pages we write  in new order for the ages where out of  many  we are one bright as a son and bold  as an eagle a nation of all people  by all people for all people  amanda gorman has written poems for  historic occasions  now she's making history herself  how cool is this um it's amazing  especially at my age no one really gets  to say at 22 i am the inaugural poet  gorman says she researched the speeches  of abraham lincoln  and dr martin luther king jr as she  began writing  and then on the wednesday in which we  saw the insurrection at the capitol  that was the day that the poem really  came to life and i really put petal to  the metal  did that change the poem for you  definitely i wanted it to be a message  of  hope and unity and i think that  wednesday for me really just  underscored how much that was needed but  to not  turn a blind eye to the cracks that  really need to be filled  gorman was named the nation's first  youth poet laureate  at 19. at 16 she was youth poet laureate  of los angeles i sing a neighborhood  anthem for the silence where she was  raised by a single mom  joan an english teacher the anthem will  sing  amanda drop your eyes but do not drop  your volume  and writing became her way over a  daunting  obstacle i had a speech impediment and  so i couldn't use my voice  then i would author my voice on the page  so it's really been  a godsend and a lifeline for me how did  you overcome it  poetry and you know to put it short  spoken word became my own type of  pathology  and so once i arrived at the point in my  life in high school where i said  you know what writing my poems on the  page  isn't enough for me i have to give them  breath in life i  have to perform them as i am that was  the moment that i was able to grow  past my speech impediment gorman who had  particular trouble with the letter  r also used music as therapy  my favorite thing to practice was the  song aaron burr  sir from hamilton because it was  jam-packed with ours and i said  if i can keep up with you know leslie in  this track then i am on my way to being  able to say  this r and a poem  we wild we want we stunt  we strive we hustle we hungry we run  free here we come we  one country we ain't done see so how do  you feel when you are  performing these poems when i am on  stage i  feel electric i feel like i could  breathe fire  like i am summoning the energy not only  of myself but of  my ancestors but the point of protest  isn't winning it's holding fast to the  promise of freedom  even when fast victory isn't promised  poetry is a weapon it is an instrument  of social change  and poetry is one of the most political  arts out there because it demands that  you  rupture and destabilize the language in  which you're working with inherently  you are pushing against the status quo  and so for me  it's always existed in that tradition of  truth-telling  today she will do her truth-telling on  the steps of the united states capital  how do you prepare for something like  this one of the preparations that i do  do always whenever i perform is i  say a mantra to myself which is i am the  daughter of  black riders who are descended from  freedom fighters  who broke through chains and changed the  world they call me  and that is the way in which i prepare  myself with a beauty that needs to get  done  do you say that inside your head or do  you say it aloud  if i can and i'm not going to scare  anybody off i always say it out loud so  i'm usually like in a bathroom or a  dressing room  but if you know backstage and i imagine  if i'm like standing next to  joe biden i might like keep it  have here worry about craziness i don't  want secret service to think you know  amanda's gone  nobody mayday i know she is so amazing  amanda says she battled that speech  impediment all the way into college she  graduated harvard in the spring she's  working on a bunch of books she's got a  children's book called change things  that comes out in september  long term plans 2036 running for  president she says i believe hitting me  says that she already has a hashtag  commanda in chief i like it  listen i got a great story for you  anthony because i told oprah that you  were doing her yeah  oprah said she reached out to amanda  because when maya did her speech  uh at the inaugural bill clinton right  oprah bought maya a coat  that's a pretty blue coat so she reached  out to amanda and said i'd like to  carry on the tradition amanda said i  already have a coat it's in my favorite  color  so oprah got her these gold hoop  earrings and a ring that's shaped like a  bird cage  as a nod to maya i know how the how the  uh caged bird  sings yeah so today when you're watching  her we'll see that that's really  exciting

